  "answer_text": "Great question! Here's a thorough explanation:\n\n### What Is an Adam's Apple?\n\nThe Adam's apple is a prominence of the thyroid cartilage that partially encloses the larynx (the voice box). It helps protect the walls and the frontal part of the larynx, including the vocal cords, which are located directly behind it.\n\n### Do Only Men Have One?\n\nAdam's apples are found on both women and men \u2014 they just show up more prominently in men. Both men and women have the same structures in their throats, including the thyroid cartilage, but in women, it tends to be smaller and less noticeable.\n\n### Why Is It Larger in Men?\n\nThe main reason comes down to **puberty and testosterone**:\n\n1. **Hormonal differences:** The Adam's apple is about the same size in males and females until puberty. Once puberty starts, hormones \u2014 especially testosterone \u2014 in males may cause their vocal cords to grow bigger. The thyroid cartilage may therefore stick out a bit more to protect these larger vocal cords.\n\n2. **Structural growth:** The structural size of the thyroid cartilage in males tends to increase during puberty, and the laryngeal protuberance becomes more anteriorly focused.\n\n3. **Shape differences:** There is also a difference in the shape of the laryngeal prominence between males and females. In males, the sides of the laryngeal prominence protrude at an almost 90\u00b0 angle, while in females it is at a more obtuse 120\u00b0 angle, making it appear less visible.\n\n4. **Female larynx position:** In females, the laryngeal protuberance sits on the upper edge of the thyroid cartilage, and the larynx tends to be smaller in size, so the \"bump\" caused by the protrusion is much less visible or not discernible.\n\n### Why Does It Affect Voice?\n\nSome people develop more cartilage around the vocal cords, or they have a larger voice box. People with a larger Adam's apple tend to have a deeper voice than people with a smaller one. This is why men's voices typically deepen significantly during puberty.\n\n### Can Women Have a Noticeable Adam's Apple?\n\nYes! Many women display an externally visible protrusion of the thyroid cartilage to varying degrees, which is usually minor, and this should not normally be viewed as a medical disorder. Women who have higher levels of testosterone, either naturally or due to medical conditions, may experience more masculine traits, including a larger larynx and a more noticeable Adam's apple.\n\nIn summary, men have larger Adam's apples primarily because testosterone during puberty causes the larynx and surrounding cartilage to grow much more significantly in males than in females.",
